from pathlib import Path
import json
p=Path('content/networks/02-ip-routing/ip-routing-basics.questions.json');q=json.loads(p.read_text())
q[0]['prompt']+=' Assume these are eligible installed routes in the selected forwarding table, with no overriding policy.'
q[1]['prompt']+=' Assume ordinary Ethernet unicast forwarding without tunnelling, source routing or rewriting.'
q[1]['options'][1]['explanation']='In this case the gateway IP selects the Ethernet next hop; it does not replace B as the packet destination.'
q[1]['options'][2]['explanation']='The selected next hop is R1, so A resolves R1\'s local MAC. B is beyond the routed link.'
q[1]['workedExample']=q[1]['workedExample'].replace('so it uses the default route via R1','and its selected route points via R1')
q[2]['options'][0]['explanation']='The selected route names 192.168.1.1 as next hop. Proxy ARP can answer for nonlocal IPs in other configurations, but it does not change this lookup.'
q[3]['prompt']+=' Assume next hops are reachable and candidates are otherwise eligible.'
q[3]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct. During route selection the static /16 beats the OSPF /16. Both the winning /16 and the separate /24 are installed. LPM then chooses Z for .5.5 and X for .9.9.'
q[3]['options'][3]['explanation']='In this stated default Cisco comparison, static distance 1 is preferred to OSPF 110 for the same prefix. That does not mean every configured static route is usable.'
q[3]['workedExample']='First select routes for each prefix: static distance 1 beats OSPF 110 for 10.1/16. The separate OSPF 10.1.5/24 is also installed. At packet lookup, 10.1.5.5 matches /24 and uses Z; 10.1.9.9 matches the selected /16 and uses X. Protocol state may retain the losing candidate; exact database placement is implementation-dependent. AD is not reconsidered for every forwarded packet.'
q[4]['prompt']+=' Assume these are the only default candidates and the static next hop remains resolvable.'
q[4]['options'][2]['explanation']='Multiple configured or learned candidates can coexist. Selection determines which usable forwarding entries are installed.'
q[4]['workedExample']='For the same default prefix, OSPF distance 110 beats static 250. If OSPF withdraws its route and the static remains resolvable, the static can be installed. When eligible OSPF returns it is preferred again. An upstream failure does not necessarily cause immediate withdrawal; detection and tracking matter.'
q[5]['prompt']='An ordinary IPv4 router forwards a unicast TCP packet between Ethernet links, without NAT, tunnels, fragmentation, options processing or other rewriting. Which actions are required? Select all that apply.'
q[5]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. Forwarding decrements TTL by at least one; expiry prevents forwarding and normally prompts an ICMP error subject to suppression/rate rules.'
q[5]['options'][1]['text']='Update the IPv4 header checksum, possibly incrementally'
q[5]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct. The header checksum must remain consistent with the TTL change.'
q[5]['workedExample']='Under these explicit assumptions, forwarding changes TTL and its IPv4 header checksum and constructs an outgoing Ethernet frame for the selected next hop. IP source/destination and TCP bytes remain unchanged, and TTL is not part of the TCP pseudo-header, so the TCP checksum does not need updating. Other router functions can change additional fields outside this scenario.'
q[6]['prompt']='Consider this deliberately incomplete lookup variant. Its `routes` list has already been rebuilt without the default and contains only 10/8, 10.1/16 and 10.1.2/24. What happens for `lookup("8.8.8.8")`?\n\n```python\ndef lookup(addr):\n    a = ip.ip_address(addr)\n    hits = [(n.prefixlen, hop)\n            for n, hop in routes\n            if a in n]\n    return max(hits)[1]\n```'
q[6]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct. No route matches, so max receives an empty list and raises ValueError. The corrected lesson version returns None for this case.'
q[6]['workedExample']='The rebuilt routes contain no prefix matching 8.8.8.8, so hits is empty and max raises before indexing. The lesson\'s corrected function checks this case and returns None. Removing an entry from the original table alone would not change a separately constructed routes list. A router without a route generally discards the packet and may report ICMP subject to suppression and rate limits.'
q[7]['prompt']='A switch uses stable per-flow 5-tuple hashing over four equal-cost uplinks. What is a principal reason to choose this instead of per-packet round-robin across paths with different delays?'
q[7]['options'][2]['text']='It reduces cross-path reordering that can trigger spurious transport loss recovery'
q[7]['options'][2]['explanation']='Correct. Path affinity reduces one source of reordering; TCP can tolerate some reordering, and the exact recovery response depends on the algorithm.'
q[7]['workedExample']='Packets sent over different paths can arrive out of order because delays differ. Stable flow hashing tends to keep a flow on one next hop while the path set and hash inputs remain stable, reducing that cause of reordering. It does not guarantee order during path changes, and elephant flows can still produce imbalance. Per-packet ECMP is possible; this is a design trade-off, not an IP prohibition.'
q[8]['prompt']='A plain binary trie holds any valid IPv4 prefix set. What is the maximum number of bit-consuming edges followed from its root in one lookup? Do not count the root as an edge.'
q[8]['options'][2]['explanation']='A plain binary trie follows address bits; it does not use one hash lookup. TCAM timing is hardware-dependent.'
q[8]['options'][3]['text']='32, one per address bit'
q[8]['workedExample']='The root has depth 0 and an IPv4 /32 node has depth 32. A lookup follows at most 32 edges, visiting at most 33 nodes including the root. The algorithmic bound is O(W) for address width W. Memory layout, caching and compression determine actual accesses and time; they are not necessarily one fixed-cost read per abstract node.'
q[9]['options'][0]['explanation']='The question specifies a first-match priority TCAM. This model needs an ordering/priority scheme that implements LPM; not every hardware design has identical ordering semantics.'
q[9]['options'][2]['explanation']='Changing entry order changes this priority model\'s chosen match; it does not turn the specified parallel matching operation into a sequential scan.'
q[10]['prompt']='A Linux server has eth0 (10.0.0.5/24, default via 10.0.0.1) and eth1 (192.168.50.5/24, connected route only). A SYN from 192.168.60.20 to 192.168.50.5 arrives via eth1. Captures confirm it is accepted and the SYN-ACK leaves eth0, but the required return path is via 192.168.50.1. No overriding policy applies. Which change can direct replies from 192.168.50.5 through eth1?'
q[10]['options'][0]['text']='Install a source-specific routing table with the eth1 connected route and gateway default, plus an appropriately prioritised rule for source 192.168.50.5'
q[10]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. The current default selects eth0. A complete source-specific table can select eth1 for replies with this source; a destination-specific route is another option for a narrower requirement.'
q[10]['options'][2]['explanation']='The captures establish the egress choice. Raising TTL does not select the desired next hop.'
q[10]['workedExample']='The destination 192.168.60.20 matches neither connected subnet, so the main default selects eth0. For the specified source-specific requirement, an illustrative configuration is:\n\n```sh\nip route add 192.168.50.0/24 \\\n  dev eth1 scope link table 100\nip route add default \\\n  via 192.168.50.1 dev eth1 table 100\nip rule add priority 1000 \\\n  from 192.168.50.5/32 table 100\n```\n\nAssume table 100 and priority 1000 are unused and no earlier rule overrides the lookup. Validate the result with `ip route get` including the source. The table needs gateway reachability, not just a bare default. Firewall and reverse-path settings are separate checks; captures, not missing replies alone, established this route choice.'
q[11]['prompt']='Which statements about hardware forwarding capacity and Cisco\'s documented 2014 TCAM-exhaustion issue are accurate? Select all that apply.'
q[11]['options'][0]['text']='Some documented Catalyst 6500/7600 configurations had a finite default FIB TCAM allocation and could enter an exception state when exceeded'
q[11]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. Cisco FN63866 documents the affected configurations and default 512k allocation; this is not a universal limit for all routers.'
q[11]['options'][1]['text']='Cisco reported that some entries could be software switched, with possible elevated CPU usage and connectivity impact'
q[11]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct for the documented exception condition. This does not prove every overflowing router uses the same fallback.'
q[11]['options'][2]['explanation']='TCAM provides parallel masked matching, but its cost, power and timing depend on the design. It is not universally cheap, low-power or a one-cycle complete forwarding pipeline.'
q[11]['options'][3]['text']='Correct aggregation can reduce the prefix entries a receiving router must represent'
q[11]['options'][3]['explanation']='Correct. Actual FIB savings depend on accepted routes, remaining more-specifics, address families and hardware encoding; every advertisement need not become one entry everywhere.'
q[11]['workedExample']='Cisco\'s field notice documents particular finite TCAM allocations and exception behaviour, including software switching of some entries. Routing visibility and hardware allocation are deployment-specific. Proper aggregation can reduce represented prefixes, but advertised aggregates must preserve intended reachability and cannot be treated as an unconditional fix for every capacity problem.'
q[11]['link']={'title':'Cisco FN63866: TCAM exhaustion','url':'https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/field-notices/638/fn63866.html'}
p.write_text(json.dumps(q,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
